      "question_type": "mcq",
      "rationale": "Perspective communicates form, but technical verification requires the rest of the drawing/document set.",
      "explanation": "Incorrect. The VPs move away from each other as one facade becomes more frontal and the other more receded. Correct. You understood how station point movement affects VP locations. Incorrect. Vanishing point positions change relative to the observer's station point. Incorrect. While the left facade becomes more prominent, the vanishing point behavior is more specific: the left VP moves left (away from center), and the right VP moves right. As the observer moves left, the viewing angle changes. The left facade becomes more frontal (less receded), so its vanishing point moves farther left; the right facade becomes more receded, so its VP moves farther right. The two VPs move *away* from each other in the direction of the observer's movement. Vanishing points are not fixed in space; they change with the observer's position (station point). Moving the station point closer to the building's corner brings the two VPs closer together and toward the center. This is how perspective composition is controlled.",
