      "question_type": "mcq",
      "rationale": "PD 1096 RIRR names bird's-eye and eye-level vantage points.",
      "explanation": "Incorrect. 1VP has one vanishing point and is used for frontal views where one face is parallel to the picture plane. Correct. You identified the 2VP configuration correctly. Incorrect. 3VP would include a third (vertical) vanishing point, making vertical lines converge as well. Incorrect. Axonometric is not a true perspective; receding lines remain parallel and do not converge. Two visible building facades with two vanishing points on the horizon line define 2VP perspective. This is the standard view for showing a building corner and is the most common exterior perspective type in architectural practice. 2VP perspective is most common for building exterior views. Count the vanishing points on the horizon line: one point = 1VP, two points = 2VP. This is a quick identification method.",
