      "question_type": "mcq",
      "rationale": "The source-backed issue in this batch is documentation and vantage point, not full projection theory.",
      "explanation": "Incorrect. Eye-level view produces 2VP perspective (left and right facades converge on the horizon). Incorrect. A bird's eye view has a third VP *below* the horizon line, not above. Correct. You identified the worm's eye view creating 3VP perspective with vertical convergence. Incorrect. This would also produce 2VP (unless the viewpoint is elevated or depressed). When viewing a tall building from ground level, looking upward, the vertical edges appear to converge toward a point above the horizon line. This creates a third vanishing point. The view emphasizes height and can create a dramatic effect but introduces distortion that may not always appear realistic. 3VP perspective with upward convergence = looking up at a tall object from ground level (worm's eye). This is used for dramatic effect but can appear distorted. Use 2VP perspective for most architectural presentations unless dramatic height emphasis is the goal.",
